1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's one of the Allies' most feared and lethal killers of World War II.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
An aircraft with a sting in its tail and a two-ton bomb load.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Revolutionary in design, it was the fastest of its time and built almost entirely out of wood.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
It took the fight to the enemy's door and swept across Europe.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
The Mosquito, a legend in its own lifetime.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Next on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,900
The best aircraft are often the fastest.

8
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:50,700
The pursuit of speed has been part of the appeal of aviation from its earliest days.

9
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,640
And often it is the most innovative and radical designs that break speed records.

10
00:00:56,641 --> 00:01:03,880
the Lockheed Blackbird uses new metals that came out of the space race and flies at over

11
00:01:03,881 --> 00:01:14,940
three times the speed of sound throughout the 1930s one company in Britain was also

12
00:01:14,941 --> 00:01:20,280
obsessed with speed and it was one man's dream to use a radical material to build

13
00:01:20,281 --> 00:01:24,040
the fastest aircraft in the world.

14
00:01:24,041 --> 00:01:29,040
The material was wood, and his name was Jeffrey de Havilland.

15
00:01:29,041 --> 00:01:32,040
He was so very determined, anything he wanted to do,

16
00:01:32,041 --> 00:01:34,200
he got on with it and did it.

17
00:01:34,201 --> 00:01:38,160
A real leader of people, not by driving them,

18
00:01:38,161 --> 00:01:41,620
but leading them from the front.

19
00:01:41,621 --> 00:01:44,200
As early as the 1930s, de Havilland

20
00:01:44,201 --> 00:01:46,100
knew that lightweight wooden aircraft

21
00:01:46,101 --> 00:01:47,680
were the key to greater speeds.

22
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:56,280
In 1934, his twin-engine comet won the London to Melbourne, Australia air race.

23
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:03,380
With speeds of over 200 miles per hour, the aircraft flew halfway around the world in just 77 hours.

24
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:05,520
A ship would take four weeks.

25
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:13,660
From his experience building the comet, de Havilland visualized building a super-fast, high-precision bomber.

26
00:02:13,661 --> 00:02:24,520
the Haviland was convinced that with the rise of the German Luftwaffe in the mid-1930s and the terror

27
00:02:24,521 --> 00:02:29,140
they inflicted during the Spanish Civil War Britain would soon need all the heavy-duty

28
00:02:29,141 --> 00:02:41,800
airpower it could produce it took the British quite a while to realize that the Germans had

29
00:02:41,801 --> 00:02:46,640
innovative designs monoplane fighters but more importantly they had medium

30
00:02:46,641 --> 00:02:51,460
bombers that were being developed and they had in effect tested them in the

31
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:57,300
Spanish Civil War 1936 onwards it's a huge wake-up call

32
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:02,140
at the time the British Air Ministry believed that the future of bombing lay

33
00:03:02,141 --> 00:03:06,280
in huge heavily armed conventional bombers such as the Lancaster and

34
00:03:06,281 --> 00:03:08,560
Halifax

35
00:03:08,561 --> 00:03:15,160
radical new designs of lightweight aircraft were ignored but Jeffrey de

36
00:03:15,161 --> 00:03:20,760
Havilland was not deterred and continued working on designs for his new bomber

37
00:03:20,761 --> 00:03:26,380
his goal was to construct a cheap easy to build aircraft powered by two Rolls

38
00:03:26,381 --> 00:03:32,360
Royce Merlin engines it would have no guns no armor plating could carry up to

39
00:03:32,361 --> 00:03:38,820
4,000 pounds of explosive and reach a speed of nearly 400 miles per hour, faster than

40
00:03:38,821 --> 00:03:43,160
any other aircraft in the world.

41
00:03:43,161 --> 00:03:51,140
But the most amazing concept was to build a warplane made entirely of wood.

42
00:03:51,141 --> 00:03:56,700
The aerodynamicists and the military men at that time ridiculed the idea asking, how can

43
00:03:56,701 --> 00:03:59,320
you build a modern bomber out of wood?

44
00:03:59,321 --> 00:04:03,700
would it carry bombs and how would it protect itself but the Haviland was

45
00:04:03,701 --> 00:04:09,640
prepared to answer it wouldn't need to protect itself its defense would be

46
00:04:09,641 --> 00:04:16,600
speed as for strength pound-for-pound timber has similar structural properties

47
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:23,260
as aluminum and steel there's a shortage in Britain the night late 1930s of

48
00:04:23,261 --> 00:04:28,840
metal workers of people skilled in in the new technologies of fighter

49
00:04:28,841 --> 00:04:34,300
airplanes of bomber airplanes what you did have there was a nation of cabinet

50
00:04:34,301 --> 00:04:39,400
builders of woodworkers of individual small craftsmen but more importantly

51
00:04:39,401 --> 00:04:44,020
you can actually mobilize all of these people and to build it and you can make

52
00:04:44,021 --> 00:04:47,840
it in effect a cottage industry

53
00:04:47,841 --> 00:04:53,840
September 1939 with war breaking out in Europe de Havilland is at last given a

54
00:04:53,841 --> 00:04:55,840
green light to build a prototype aircraft.

55
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:02,540
Immediately, the de Havilland design team goes into overdrive.

56
00:05:02,541 --> 00:05:05,620
Led by Ronald Bishop, they move into a 17th century

57
00:05:05,621 --> 00:05:07,440
manor house called Salisbury Hall.

58
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,680
This remote house was chosen to keep the new aircraft away

59
00:05:13,681 --> 00:05:15,140
from prying enemy eyes.

60
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:22,480
By October 1939, work begins by building a mock-up

61
00:05:22,481 --> 00:05:25,420
aircraft in the manor's enormous kitchen.

62
00:05:25,421 --> 00:05:28,220
A hangar disguised as a barn is constructed

63
00:05:28,221 --> 00:05:30,220
at the western side of the moat.

64
00:05:30,221 --> 00:05:32,660
We were working often periods of time,

65
00:05:32,661 --> 00:05:34,880
six and a half days a week.

66
00:05:34,881 --> 00:05:38,060
And at some periods, from seven o'clock in the morning

67
00:05:38,061 --> 00:05:39,400
to seven o'clock at night.

68
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,380
But they are racing against the clock.

69
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:49,440
In the spring of 1940, Germany conquers France

70
00:05:49,441 --> 00:05:52,440
and the British Army is swept off the beaches of Dunkirk.

71
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:57,540
The Germans are poised for an invasion of Britain.

72
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:03,020
Every effort is focused on holding off the Germans,

73
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:06,220
and de Havilland's Wonderplane seems destined

74
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:08,360
to never leave the drawing board.

75
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:13,860
Air Marshal Sir Wilfred Freeman was the only member

76
00:06:13,861 --> 00:06:16,500
of RAF High Command to support the Mosquito.

77
00:06:16,501 --> 00:06:20,820
As a result, the project was initially known as Freeman's Folly.

78
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:23,300
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

79
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:30,060
June 1940.

80
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:33,560
The German army has advanced to the shores of the English Channel

81
00:06:33,561 --> 00:06:35,640
and is getting ready to invade Britain.

82
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:40,120
In just six months, the Nazis have conquered Poland,

83
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,920
Norway, Denmark, Belgium, and France.

84
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:53,500
Britain is the only country left to face the onslaught of Nazi Germany.

85
00:06:53,501 --> 00:07:03,680
With Britain fighting for its life, priority is given to production of existing types of

86
00:07:03,681 --> 00:07:06,540
combat aircraft.

87
00:07:06,541 --> 00:07:12,420
Jeffrey de Havilland's radical new wooden bomber looks condemned to be scrapped.

88
00:07:12,421 --> 00:07:15,140
de Havilland is not a man to take no for an answer.

89
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:22,680
Sir Jeffrey's words are, well, they may not want it now, but they will want it.

90
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:27,460
de Havilland persuades the air ministry to let him continue

91
00:07:27,461 --> 00:07:31,920
as long as he does not use any of the vital materials required for the war effort.

92
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:40,720
Throughout the summer of 1940, as Britain fights for its life against the German Luftwaffe in the Battle of Britain,

93
00:07:40,721 --> 00:07:43,720
De Havilland and his team work on the wooden bomber.

94
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:51,060
Finally, in December 1940,

95
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:54,320
the aircraft is ready to be shown to the men from the ministry.

96
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:57,360
It is called the Mosquito.

97
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:00,560
De Havilland has done the impossible.

98
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:03,480
Using scraps from the British military machine,

99
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:06,380
he created the fastest aircraft in the world.

100
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:08,540
We knew it would fly.

101
00:08:08,541 --> 00:08:11,520
We were absolutely confident it would.

102
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:15,620
And the question is how much better than the Spitfire would be,

103
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:16,580
because we said it would be.

104
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:17,820
And it was.

105
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:19,860
20 miles an hour faster.

106
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:25,520
The Air Ministry is so impressed with its performance

107
00:08:25,521 --> 00:08:28,500
that they place an order for 150 mosquitoes.

108
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:33,880
It is an amazing contract for an untried and untested aircraft.

109
00:08:33,881 --> 00:08:46,660
this one was an impossible task it's a wonderful thing to change from I don't want it and so give

110
00:08:46,661 --> 00:08:54,940
us more and more but the Haviland's vision of construction answers the call enabling the

111
00:08:54,941 --> 00:09:01,960
mosquito to be built in vast numbers a plan that is as simple in practice as it is visionary in

112
00:09:01,961 --> 00:09:11,980
conception the aircraft would be built like a model plane with wood plywood glue and screws

113
00:09:11,981 --> 00:09:19,240
over 400 companies around Britain with wood craftsmen would make the various sections

114
00:09:19,241 --> 00:09:25,200
the fuselage would be built in two halves before being bonded together

115
00:09:25,201 --> 00:09:32,600
the wing would be assembled in one piece and then transported to the main factory for the final

116
00:09:32,601 --> 00:09:41,320
assembly in all over six tons of wood and nearly 50,000 brass screws are used in each aircraft

117
00:09:41,321 --> 00:09:48,980
at 40 feet long with a wingspan of 54 feet a legend is born

118
00:09:48,981 --> 00:09:57,920
with a speed of 400 miles per hour faster than any aircraft in the world the mosquito is ready

119
00:09:57,921 --> 00:10:06,440
to do battle the RAF views this plane is too valuable to let just anybody fly it only the

120
00:10:06,441 --> 00:10:12,200
most experienced crews are selected but for many of these men the mosquito powered by its two

121
00:10:12,201 --> 00:10:16,600
mighty Merlin engines is unlike anything they had ever flown before.

122
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:23,360
The purr of a Merlin is something, something to experience, it really is.

123
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:30,120
And then we taxied out and took off.

124
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:34,880
There you were, you suddenly look at the altimeter,

125
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:37,680
the one minute you were down at 1,000 feet,

126
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:39,800
the next minute you're up at 30,000.

127
00:10:39,801 --> 00:10:45,040
She would climb at 2,500 feet a minute and that was really going some.

128
00:10:45,041 --> 00:10:50,080
You had to get into a jet to fly anything faster than that.

129
00:10:50,081 --> 00:10:53,900
If you started to mishandle her, pushing and pulling, in no time at all you would over

130
00:10:53,901 --> 00:10:56,260
control and she would be like a mosquito.

131
00:10:56,261 --> 00:11:00,880
She would sting you.

132
00:11:00,881 --> 00:11:06,640
In July 1941, the first mosquitoes to go into service are delivered as photoreconnaissance

133
00:11:06,641 --> 00:11:07,840
units.

134
00:11:07,841 --> 00:11:15,760
It has taken just 22 months from the first drawings to delivery.

135
00:11:15,761 --> 00:11:20,880
Unarmed, bristling with up to five cameras, these aircraft are soon sweeping all over

136
00:11:20,881 --> 00:11:26,900
Europe, evading anti-aircraft guns and German fighters.

137
00:11:26,901 --> 00:11:29,400
The Germans have nowhere to hide.

138
00:11:29,401 --> 00:11:32,520
Nothing is safe from the mosquitoes' prying eyes.

139
00:11:32,521 --> 00:11:34,740
Photoreconnaissance was vital.

140
00:11:34,741 --> 00:11:40,540
We're talking the days before satellites, the days when human intelligence on the ground was limited.

141
00:11:40,541 --> 00:11:42,940
It was difficult to get back good information.

142
00:11:42,941 --> 00:11:50,240
Before every single bomber command raid over enemy targets, they had to take pictures of the targets.

143
00:11:50,241 --> 00:11:53,940
In fact, they had to report the weather as well.

144
00:11:53,941 --> 00:11:56,140
So the Mosquito was an ideal airplane for that.

145
00:11:56,141 --> 00:11:58,740
In fact, you could say probably it was the best aircraft for this.

146
00:11:58,741 --> 00:12:07,060
But it is as a bomber that the Mosquito comes into its own.

147
00:12:07,061 --> 00:12:12,640
During the early part of World War II, bombing was a case of hit or miss.

148
00:12:12,641 --> 00:12:18,380
Aircraft flying at 16,000 feet would drop their bombs in huge numbers, relying on luck,

149
00:12:18,381 --> 00:12:25,300
weather and flying skill to hit their targets.

150
00:12:25,301 --> 00:12:32,740
But in 1942, the Mosquito bomber version changes all that.

151
00:12:32,741 --> 00:12:37,380
Relying solely on speed and height to outrun the enemy fighters, Mosquitoes can drop their

152
00:12:37,381 --> 00:12:41,060
bombs with pinpoint accuracy.

153
00:12:41,061 --> 00:12:47,800
Soon Mosquitoes with their payload of 4,000 pounds are an integral part of bombing missions.

154
00:12:47,801 --> 00:12:54,120
But the Mosquito is even more deadly once the pilots perfect the art of fast, low-level

155
00:12:54,121 --> 00:12:59,118
daylight raids. We had to be under 50 feet to be under the radar.

156
00:12:59,119 --> 00:13:01,040
That's the whole point of

157
00:13:01,041 --> 00:13:04,173
low-level operations, so the Germans don't know you're coming.

158
00:13:04,174 --> 00:13:05,740
And then, of course, you had to

159
00:13:05,741 --> 00:13:10,420
go low over the target as possible, so that you took them hopefully by surprise.

160
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:19,820
Some daylight raids were flown at such low altitudes that mosquitoes would often come

161
00:13:19,821 --> 00:13:22,220
back with strange souvenirs from their mission.

162
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:27,820
Many Amosie pilots come back with telephone wires draped round his tail wheel.

163
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:32,640
I know I've come back with branches of trees that weren't planted in England.

164
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:38,480
Mosquito squadrons were now becoming more and more daring in their attacks,

165
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:43,420
none more so than the daylight raid against the Dutch Philips Radio Factory in Eindhoven.

166
00:13:43,421 --> 00:13:50,080
intelligence discovered that the Germans were using this factory for research into

167
00:13:50,081 --> 00:13:58,160
radar countermeasures it must be destroyed December 6th 1942 10 mosquitoes go for their target

168
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:10,360
we went low level down the shelter in Holland

169
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:17,020
And then just then we were approaching a place I think called Turnhout.

170
00:14:18,620 --> 00:14:22,940
And at that point we had to climb up to a thousand feet.

171
00:14:25,020 --> 00:14:31,980
And then we turned to port and up came the factory and went down in a screaming dive

172
00:14:31,981 --> 00:14:33,980
and dropped our bomb straight into it.

173
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:53,100
Racing for home, the mosquitoes are caught in German anti-aircraft fire or flak.

174
00:14:53,101 --> 00:14:55,300
Over the North Sea, disaster strikes.

175
00:14:55,301 --> 00:15:01,020
The chap who decided to follow me, because he was on his first trip and he thought he'd

176
00:15:01,021 --> 00:15:06,180
follow an experienced pilot, and I'm afraid he caught a bit of flak because he, my new

177
00:15:06,181 --> 00:15:13,020
cameraman, suddenly called out looking backwards, uh, God, he's gone into the sea.

178
00:15:13,021 --> 00:15:15,660
I turned around and went back to the sea.

179
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:19,880
And there was no dinghy or anything, just a cauldron of boiling water.

180
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:26,380
Just six weeks later, on January 30th, 1943,

181
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:29,500
the mosquitoes go on another audacious raid

182
00:15:29,501 --> 00:15:32,320
on the 10th anniversary of Hitler's coming to power.

183
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:37,980
Intelligence learns that there is to be a major Nazi rally in Berlin,

184
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,980
led by Reich propaganda minister Joseph Goebbels

185
00:15:40,981 --> 00:15:42,700
and Reich marshal Hermann Goering.

186
00:15:43,020 --> 00:15:47,520
It will be broadcast on German radio.

187
00:15:47,521 --> 00:15:50,620
The opportunity is too good to miss.

188
00:15:50,621 --> 00:15:59,000
The goal is not to hit the rally, but to create chaos and interrupt the broadcast.

189
00:15:59,001 --> 00:16:06,960
Three mosquitoes hit Berlin at 11 a.m., interrupting Goering's broadcast.

190
00:16:06,961 --> 00:16:11,200
At 4 p.m., a second group hits, interrupting Goebbels.

191
00:16:11,201 --> 00:16:15,440
For the Allies, it is a major propaganda success.

192
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:23,260
The Mosquito delivered to the Allies, particularly to the British, propaganda coups right the way through its service history.

193
00:16:23,261 --> 00:16:31,580
And all done by a small wooden airplane that the Germans had written off in their propaganda as being some sort of antique.

194
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:35,800
Leader Goering has heard to say,

195
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:38,660
It makes me furious when I see the Mosquito.

196
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:41,060
I turn green and yellow with envy.

197
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:46,800
The British knocked together a beautiful wooden aircraft that every piano factory over there is building.

198
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:49,860
They give it a speed, which they have increased again.

199
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:56,240
But if the Mosquitoes were spoiling Goering's day, the Allies know that to spoil the Nazis' day,

200
00:16:56,580 --> 00:17:02,600
the Mosquitoes are going to have to bomb Berlin again and again until they smash it into submission.

201
00:17:02,601 --> 00:17:13,520
In an era of metal planes, the Mosquito excelled in so many different combat roles that it was nicknamed the Wooden Wonder.

202
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:16,100
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

203
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:26,020
Since entering service, the Wooden Mosquito has been thrown into battle with devastating results.

204
00:17:26,021 --> 00:17:34,600
whatever role it is asked to do the mosquito answers the challenge always ready to take

205
00:17:34,601 --> 00:17:43,180
the fight to Hitler's doorstep by mid-1943 the mosquito is bombing Berlin on such a regular

206
00:17:43,181 --> 00:17:50,980
basis that the missions are referred to as the Berlin Express but for the mosquito crews nothing

207
00:17:50,981 --> 00:17:52,640
is ever just a routine mission.

208
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:58,520
We would take off after the Lancasters,

209
00:17:58,521 --> 00:18:00,980
perhaps two hours after the Lancasters had gone.

210
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:07,800
And it was at that time that I really felt lonely.

211
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:12,800
It was a very lonely feeling.

212
00:18:12,801 --> 00:18:17,440
It was at that point that you could start thinking

213
00:18:17,441 --> 00:18:19,200
about what you were doing.

214
00:18:19,201 --> 00:18:26,200
You had to rely entirely on the navigator who had radar aids and things like that.

215
00:18:26,201 --> 00:18:31,200
But then with the night closing in on you,

216
00:18:31,201 --> 00:18:37,200
suddenly you suddenly came to realize that you'd left your homeland behind

217
00:18:37,201 --> 00:18:43,200
and you were heading for, you knew not what.

218
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:50,560
After two hours, the much faster mosquitoes catch up with the slow lumbering Lancasters heading for Germany.

219
00:18:53,380 --> 00:19:00,340
Flying at 25,000 feet, the Mosquito crews look down on the procession of bombers 7,000 feet below them.

220
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:05,220
And then you would probably be treated to a site you would rather not see,

221
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:10,060
which was the German night fighter force intercepting the Lancasters.

222
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:15,100
when it's true to say that once the German night fighters found the bomber

223
00:19:15,101 --> 00:19:18,940
stream they didn't let go and they'd be there

224
00:19:18,941 --> 00:19:25,280
hammering away at the flanks of the heavies and you'd see a sheet of flame

225
00:19:25,281 --> 00:19:32,500
as a lank caught fire and then blew up and this would be the melancholy

226
00:19:32,501 --> 00:19:39,100
procession to the target and out of it too it's just awful but it is not only

227
00:19:39,101 --> 00:19:41,760
the night fighters that they have to get past.

228
00:19:41,761 --> 00:19:44,940
The German searchlights also hunt for any Allied aircraft

229
00:19:44,941 --> 00:19:47,200
in the sky.

230
00:19:47,201 --> 00:19:52,120
The favorite thing for them to do was to pick up somebody

231
00:19:52,121 --> 00:19:54,740
